{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791286983,"nonce":"XTb+78zFEus28D4epwrb38h97Og=","message":"Landed in 56fb27e98c2bd94793f65c518b322b020bb68bb8.\n\nOverridden methods now get per-signature instances and a dispatcher preserving the argument types. Dispatcher arms use an override's matching instance when available, or adapt to its generic signature, and handle overrides inherited by grandchildren. Result types join the implementations actually reachable by that dispatcher. Arity failures preserve their TypeError.\n\nA dynamic non-method loop function reading multiple attributes of a receiver gets bounded class-specialization candidates. Its ordinary entry checks the receiver's class-kind range before unboxing it into the specialized function. None and other values run the original body. Failed specialization forwarders are excluded from these guards to avoid recursive re-entry. The existing specialization budget still applies; benchmark kernels and tier thresholds are unchanged.\n\nThe shipped SOR check now has Array2D.__getitem__(Tuple(Int, Int))-\u003eFloat and a typed override dispatcher. The real benchmark's dynamic caller enters the typed SOR loop through the class guard. Regression cases cover base/override/grandchild indexing and stores, differing return kinds, dynamic receiver fallbacks, defaults/keywords, wrong override arity, bounds exceptions and a failed class specialization.\n\nValidation on exact committed sources:\n- macOS LLVM-enabled release build: 50 conformance groups passed with LLVM off; 50 passed with ZYPY_LLVM=1. Both tuple-key AST pins and both LLVM aggregate ABI tests passed.\n- NUC x86_64: both AST pins and both LLVM aggregate ABI tests passed. The full Python --fast suite with LLVM enabled passed all 22 supported checks; pidigits is still explicitly unsupported.\n- Full SOR workload (100x100, 5000 cycles), three alternating before/after processes against parent 5f8dc2fa5e3a6001af794fdc2d647e872fabf704:\n\n| metric | before median | after median | improvement |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| kernel | 34142.323 ms | 11882.412 ms | 2.873x |\n| process wall | 34334.594 ms | 12079.144 ms | 2.842x |\n\nKernel samples before: 34214.522, 34119.051, 34142.323 ms; after: 11907.123, 11823.441, 11882.412 ms. Both binaries pass the shipped SOR checksum against CPython. NUC i5-1250P, PyPy 7.3.20/Python 3.11.13, LLVM 21, normal tiered path.\n\nThe subsequent suite screen gives SOR 11940.474 ms vs PyPy 205.662 ms (58.06x slower), wall 12150.960 vs 242.541 ms. The remaining requested _idx(Int, Int)-\u003eInt pin on the actual benchmark is moved to 64deb1b08da897076960b39b8c13ece0fa8d667ac464b5b6243bdb8bd8e47785: constructor dimensions from map(eval, args) leave width/height physically Object, so _idx's coordinates are Int but its result/arithmetic remain Object. The statically integer-dimension fixture pins the native Int result. Closing the completed dispatch gap does not claim the SOR performance gap is closed.","files":null}]}